<section class="reading-section" id="notes">
  <div class="reading-label"><p class="eyebrow">About the model</p><h2>The rules change<br />the answer.</h2></div>
  <div class="reading-copy">
    <p>Workers keep their vote after losing a job. They vote for the policy that improves their own expected income, including the cost of weaker investment. “Mathematicians first” describes the thought experiment; the model does not predict which profession comes next.</p>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>One ballot and international competition</summary>
      <p id="voting-rules">Every adult US citizen has one equal vote. Every combination in the policy menu is a complete package on one ballot: AI pace, job retention, benefit amounts, benefit formula and both tax rates. Each person chooses the fully funded package that gives their household the highest expected income utility. The package with the most votes passes only if it has more than 50% of all votes. Otherwise current taxes, benefits and AI pace remain. There is no runoff or second vote.</p>
      <p>These voters are perfectly rational and self-interested. They understand all modeled consequences over ten years, including losses from weaker investment, and give no separate value to anyone else’s welfare. Survey weights represent equal votes across the adult citizen population; income is never a voting weight. A person keeps their vote after losing a job. Job retention has no separate value beyond its income.</p>
      <p>The ballot rule requires each person to select their own favorite package. Exact income-utility ties prefer eligible current policy, then a fixed ordering. This is an explicit voting assumption: rationality alone does not uniquely determine how voters coordinate or vote strategically.</p>
      <p>In international mode, the rest of the world is one actor. It chooses the fully funded package maximizing the objective you select, given the enacted US policy. US voters choose their favorite packages given that foreign policy. The calculator looks for choices that agree with both expectations and checks the full menu for each response. It reports an unverified result if that search fails. This does not establish that a consistent pair is unique, or that none exists when the search fails.</p>
      <p>Both sides choose once, knowing the other’s choice. Their policies stay in place for ten years. If both pause AI, each expects the pause to last the decade. There is no additional agreement vote, later election or renegotiation.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>The six terms on the ballot</summary>
      <p><strong>1. AI pace.</strong> Pause AI, allow current AI pace, or accelerate AI. Checking “Pausing AI isn’t possible” removes Pause AI from all players’ choices. A pause prevents new AI exposure and AI job replacement in that economy for ten years. Current pace reaches full deployment in year ten; acceleration runs twice as fast and reaches it in year five. Full annual productivity gains arrive sooner on the accelerated path. Pausing does not eliminate other effects of foreign competition on income.</p>
      <p><strong>2. Job retention.</strong> Employers can lay off workers or retain obsolete roles at 50%, 100% or 125% of their starting wage before tax. These roles add no output. The employer bears the cost, which reduces capital income. Finding new productive work is a separate transition.</p>
      <p><strong>3. Benefit amounts.</strong> Government spending on the modeled benefits can be 0%, 50%, 100%, 150% or 200% of its survey baseline. Higher total spending does not mean every recipient receives more.</p>
      <p><strong>4. The benefit formula.</strong> Keep the current mix, pay every adult an equal amount, or link payments to prior income. “Prior income” means observed disposable income before AI, not lifetime earnings. The current mix preserves the starting distribution of benefits; losing a job does not automatically create a new unemployment-benefit payment.</p>
      <p><strong>5. Non-capital taxes.</strong> The result compares the selected tax benchmark with the survey-based US reference and shows the actual average after incomes change. A lower rate is a tax reduction. The calculation retains differences in tax burdens across income groups.</p>
      <p><strong>6. Capital taxes.</strong> The same comparison applies to capital income. These are modeled average burdens, not statutory brackets or official separate rates for “workers” and “capital owners.” A person can receive both labor and capital income.</p>
      <p>A package is eligible only if it fully funds promised retained wages, benefits and other required public spending in every year, after economic responses. No borrowing is modeled. If no eligible package wins a majority, current policy is the automatic fallback even when it becomes underfunded; any shortfall is shown explicitly. Surplus receipts fund non-transfer public spending.</p>
      <p>The tax reference covers modeled federal and state income taxes after credits, plus payroll taxes including self-employment contributions. It excludes sales and property taxes, corporate taxes and employers’ contributions for employees. Modeled net tax refunds count as benefits; this does not capture the full value of every refundable credit. All combinations in this discrete menu are evaluated. It does not cover every possible percentage.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Economic assumptions and limits</summary>
      <p>The US population and income distribution come from the Census survey. Household income is pooled and divided among adult household members. The model groups citizens by that household’s main income source, income level and their own employment status. Main sources include work, capital, public benefits and pensions. Benefit receipt and employment overlap. These group labels do not determine how anyone votes; their own projected income does.</p>
      <p>International defaults use relative GDP and bilateral trade exposure. The foreign actor uses the same income distribution as a simplifying assumption; it is not a survey of foreign voters. AI gains, displacement, return to work and responses to taxes remain adjustable assumptions. Zero foreign frontier capability leaves the foreign economy and its imports in place.</p>
      <p><strong>GDP growth and employer gains are separate.</strong> The default assumes 5% annual real GDP growth at full AI exposure. During rollout, the potential growth rate is 5% multiplied by that year’s exposure and compounds each year. There is no separate background-growth assumption. The foreign economy has its own growth setting. Taxes and investment responses can change realized growth.</p>
      <p>The employer-gain parameter divides that output. At a 50% gain, an automated role with a $100,000 prior wage gives the employer a $150,000 income claim. Keeping the employee at $100,000 leaves $50,000 before other adjustments. To keep total income consistent with the separate GDP assumption, all gross income claims are scaled to the available output before costs, taxes and retained wages are paid. This allocation rule can change workers’ and owners’ shares without changing the GDP path for a fixed policy. Changing those shares can change the policy voters choose.</p>
      <p>Current AI pace reaches full domestic deployment by year ten; accelerated AI reaches it by year five. At full deployment, 100% obsolete roles and no return to productive work means every worker is affected. Taxes and required employer pay can delay rollout within the chosen five- or ten-year schedule and reduce productive effort. They can also erode existing capital capacity through an assumed 5% annual renewal requirement. This rate is a modeling choice, not an estimate from the cited data.</p>
      <p>This is a comparison within a policy menu, not a full model of the world economy. Observed household investment income is not total company profit or wealth. The employer-funding limit and production responses remain modeling assumptions. It does not solve market prices, demand, debt, firms’ investment decisions or individual labor supply. It omits lobbying, monetary policy, AI safety and military outcomes. Changing the policy menu or voting procedure can change the result.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Equations and calculation method</summary><p>Saved results are used when available. Other assumptions are calculated by the Python service using the same model; nearby results are never substituted.</p><div id="model-method"></div></details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Why the pirate game?</summary><p>The pirate puzzle asks who can win enough votes to divide a fixed treasure. Automation changes how much is produced and who receives it. This simulator keeps the voting question and adds production, employment and government payments.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>
